{"i": 0, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"lost‑wax sprue tree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Sprue-Tree\n\n'Twas brillig, and the waxen sprue\nDid gimbal down the molten chute;\nAll mimsy were the sprong-blobs too,\nAnd the mould-core, a-hoot.\n\n\"Beware the Sprue-Tree, oh my son!\nThe branches hot, the channels deep!\nBeware the cast-out, every one,\nAnd shun the flow-hole's weep!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal mould in hand;\nLong time the Sprue-Tree foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the drag-draw stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Sprue-Tree, with its sprong-blob cast,\nCame whiffling through the green-sand wood,\nAnd burbled as it passed!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nHis vorpal mould went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its crew\nHe went a-cooling back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Sprue-Tree foe?\nBring forth the casting, bright and boon!\nO frabjous day! Calooh! Callow!\"\nHe chortled in his joy-moon.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the waxen sprue\nDid gimbal down the molten chute;\nAll mimsy were the sprong-blobs too,\nAnd the mould-core, a-hoot.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 1, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"kintsugi seam of gold\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Gleam of Glint**\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Seam, my son! The gleam\nThat glints, the flaw that shines anew!\nBeware the Kintsugold, and shun\nThe frumious patching crew!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the moldered breach he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood a while in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Kint, with eyes of fiery gleam,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it gleamed!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Kint? Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 2, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"alpenglow on scree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Alpenprowl\n\n'Twas gloaming, and the scree-rocks did\n       Gyre and gibber in the light;\nAll slippy were the slopes so hid,\n       And the skree-skrael blazed forth in might.\n\n\"Beware the Alpenprowl, my son!\n       The jaws that gleam, the claws that grip!\nBeware the Gloaming-Glower, and shun\n       The frumious Rockfall-Slip!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand:\n       Long time the frouzy foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the scree-land,\n       And stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\n       The Alpenprowl, with eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the alpen wood,\n       And burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\n       The vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its hue\n       He went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Alpenprowl?\n       Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\n       He chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas gloaming, and the scree-rocks did\n       Gyre and gibber in the light;\nAll slippy were the slopes so hid,\n       And the skree-skrael blazed forth in might.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 3, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"plane iron camber\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "### The Plancamber Gird\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Plancamber, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that snatch!\nBeware the Jubjub bird, and shun\nThe frumious Bandersnatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Plancamber, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffing through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Plancamber?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 4, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"badger knot bloom\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Grungle-Grumble\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Badger-Knot, my son!\nThe jaws that snag, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Gloom-Bloom, and shun\nThe frumious Grungle-Grumble patch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Badger-Knot, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffing through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Badger-Knot?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 5, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"summit prayer flag\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Summit Prayer-Flag**\n\n'Twas high, and the Windle-Roo did moan,\nThe crags were all grumphish and bare;\nWhen a flittery-gleam, like a spirit-stone,\nCame whirly-bopping through the air.\n\n\"Beware the Prayer-Flag! The Summit Prayer-Flag!\"\nThe guide did whimper and wail;\n\"The loops that it loops, and the flaps that it flaps,\nOh, shun its ethereal trail!\"\n\nHe took his ice-axe, a trusty old friend,\nAnd shloopingly scaled the steep rise;\nThrough snow-dune and scree-drift he did contend,\nWith valorous, glimmery eyes.\n\nAnd as he arrived at the peak's blustery lip,\nA banner, so vibrant and bright,\nDid flutter and furl with a trembly-dip,\nAcross the blue-glimmering light.\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe Windle-Roo howled and did moan;\nThe Prayer-Flag danced, a colourful hue,\nUpon the rock-topple where it was sown.\n\nAnd with its soft whisper, a whisper so dread,\nOf echoes from ages long past,\nThe climber bowed low his ice-dusted head,\nAnd felt a strange peace-spell was cast.\n\n\"And hast thou then seen the Summit Prayer-Flag?\"\nHis guide cried with watery eye.\n\"Oh, come to my arms, my beamish high-chap!\nIt blesses the peak from the sky!\"\n\n'Twas high, and the Windle-Roo did moan,\nThe crags were all grumphish and bare;\nThe flittery-gleam, like a spirit-stone,\nStill whirly-bopped through the air.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 6, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"slipjoint walk and talk\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Slipjoint Walk-and-Talk\n\n'Twas slipjoint, and the lock-blade did\n     Unspring their latchèd jaws:\nAll wibbly were the wielder's stride,\n     And the talk-words, like squaws.\n\n\"Beware the Blatherwock, my son!\n     The grip that slips, the pivot's catch!\nBeware the Flim-Flam-Fumble-Fun,\n     And shun the Screwball's scratch!\"\n\nHe deftly took his pocket-knife;\n     Long time the foe-man he had sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumbling Tree,\n     And stood in anxious thought.\n\nAnd, as in mimsy thought he stood,\n     The Blatherwock, with jaws aflame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\n     And burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\n     The slipjoint blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\n     He went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Blatherwock?\n     Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\n     He chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas slipjoint, and the lock-blade did\n     Unspring their latchèd jaws:\nAll wibbly were the wielder's stride,\n     And the talk-words, like squaws.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 7, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"rain on a tin roof\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Clatterwocky**\n\n'Twas splishy, and the slithy drops\nDid gyre and gimble on the roof:\nAll tinny were the water-plops,\nAnd the blithy rain did poof.\n\n\"Beware the Clatterwock, my son!\nThe drips that bite, the winds that catch!\nBeware the Thunderoo, and shun\nThe frumious Light-ning-flash!\"\n\nHe took his trusty bumbershoot,\nLong time the Clatterwock he sought—\nSo rested he by the tinny loot,\nAnd grumble-thought, and thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Clatterwock, with eyes of drip,\nCame whiffling through the rain-woode,\nAnd burbled as it did slip!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nHis bumbershoot went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its dew\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Clatterwock?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas splishy, and the slithy drops\nDid gyre and gimble on the roof:\nAll tinny were the water-plops,\nAnd the blithy rain did poof.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 8, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"makeready under tympan\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Tympan Ready\n\n'Twas brillick, and the burbling quads\nDid gyre and gimble in the naves;\nAll mimsey were the makeready rods,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabes.\n\n\"Beware the Tympan Ready, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Makeready blun,\nAnd shun the frumious Thatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand:\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Tympan Ready, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Tympan Ready?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillick, and the burbling quads\nDid gyre and gimble in the naves;\nAll mimsey were the makeready rods,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabes.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 9, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"modular synth patch spaghetti\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Patchwork-Snatch!\n\n'Twas bleepy, and the whirring did whine,\nAll squidgy the cables, and wrangled the knobs.\nThe oscillators hummed with a digital sign;\nA modular beast, in a tangle of throbs.\n\n\"Beware the Patchwork-Snatch, my son!\nThe jacks that bite, the circuits that seize!\nBeware the Fuzz-Wump, and shun\nThe frumious Arpeggiator’s disease!\"\n\nHe took his Voltage-Sword in hand;\nLong time the Analog-Foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Filter-land,\nAnd stood a while in thought.\n\nAnd, as in grungy thought he stood,\nThe Patchwork-Snatch, with eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the Synth-ewood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe Voltage-Sword went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its hue\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Patchwork-Snatch?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas bleepy, and the whirring did whine,\nAll squidgy the cables, and wrangled the knobs.\nThe oscillators hummed with a digital sign;\nA modular beast, in a tangle of throbs.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
